#|
Kanata configuration for a Brazilian ABNT2 keyboard on Linux. Requires kanata
1.12.0 or newer (for tap-hold-require-prior-idle). Ctrl also lives on Caps
Lock and is remapped at the kernel level, so kanata sees it as a regular left
Ctrl and passes it through untouched. Esc, Win, AltGr and Alt (the i3 mod key)
are not remapped.

There are three kinds of dual-function keys. In all of them a tap types the
letter and a hold does something else:

- Layer keys. Holding A activates the num layer (a numpad on the right hand and
  brackets on the left hand) and holding C-cedilla activates the sym layer
  (programming symbols), for as long as the key stays down. The layers are
  hold-only, there is no toggle.
- Shift keys. Holding W gives left Shift and holding O gives right Shift. Each
  one only becomes Shift when the next key comes from the opposite hand, so
  fast typing on the same hand stays a tap.
- Ctrl keys. Holding E gives left Ctrl and holding I gives right Ctrl. Unlike
  Shift, these work with keys from either hand, so same-hand combos such as
  Ctrl+W or Ctrl+D in vim are possible.

The H, J, K and L keys are not touched, so they stay free for vim.

Kanata works with physical key codes, so every symbol below is defined by the
physical key that types it on an ABNT2 layout. The operating system layout
must therefore be br with the abnt2 variant, otherwise the symbols will come
out wrong. Key names in the comments use US names, which describe the physical
position, and a few of those keys print a different character on ABNT2.
|#

(defcfg
  #|
  Also process keys that are not listed in defsrc. Without this, the tap-hold
  keys below would ignore unmapped keys, such as Shift, Space and the digits,
  when deciding between tap and hold.
  |#
  process-unmapped-keys yes

  #|
  When a tap-hold key is pressed within this many milliseconds of the previous
  key press, it is resolved as a tap right away, without waiting for the
  tap-or-hold decision. This removes the delay on A, C-cedilla, W, O, E and I
  while typing words. The cost is that a hold only works after a pause of at
  least this long since the last key. If the delay is still noticeable, raise
  it. If layers, Shift or Ctrl fire by accident while typing fast, lower it.
  |#
  tap-hold-require-prior-idle 150
)

#|
The ABNT2 layout has two keys that a US keyboard does not have: the backslash
and vertical bar key to the left of Z, and the slash and question mark key to
the left of the right Shift. They are given custom names here, built from
their Linux evdev key codes, which are 86 (KEY_102ND) and 89 (KEY_RO).
|#
(deflocalkeys-linux
  abntbs    86
  abntslash 89
)

#|
Only the three letter rows and the key right of P are listed here. Every other
key passes through unchanged thanks to process-unmapped-keys.
|#
(defsrc
  q    w    e    r    t    y    u    i    o    p    [
  a    s    d    f    g    h    j    k    l    scln
  z    x    c    v    b    n    m    ,    .    /
)

#|
Timeouts in milliseconds. The first number of a tap-hold action is the
tap-repress timeout (how soon a repeated press right after a tap still counts
as a tap). The second one is the hold timeout.

All the dual-function keys (A, C-cedilla, W, O, E and I) use tap-hold-release,
so the hold only wins when another key is pressed and released while the first
one is still down. This avoids false holds while typing fast. They follow the
global require-prior-idle value from defcfg.

If a slow tap still turns into a hold, or a fast roll turns into a layer, a
Shift or a Ctrl, raise hold-layer and hold-mod in steps of 20 to 50. If the
hold takes too long to kick in, lower them. The Shift and Ctrl keys share
hold-mod.
|#
(defvar
  tap        200
  hold-layer 300
  hold-mod   200
)

(defalias
  ;; The home row layer keys. A tap types the letter and a hold activates the
  ;; layer. A is on the left pinky and C-cedilla (scln) is on the right pinky.
  anum (tap-hold-release $tap $hold-layer a    (layer-while-held num))
  csym (tap-hold-release $tap $hold-layer scln (layer-while-held sym))

  ;; The Shift keys. Keys listed in parentheses are the ones that trigger the
  ;; tap right away, so only a key from the opposite hand can turn the press
  ;; into Shift. W is on the left hand, so its list holds the left hand keys.
  ;; O is on the right hand, so its list holds the right hand keys.
  wsft (tap-hold-release-keys $tap $hold-mod w lsft
         (q e r t a s d f g z x c v b 1 2 3 4 5 grv tab))
  osft (tap-hold-release-keys $tap $hold-mod o rsft
         (y u i p [ h j k l scln n m , . / 6 7 8 9 0 - =))

  ;; The Ctrl keys. There is no list of keys here, so a key from either hand
  ;; can turn the press into Ctrl, as long as it is pressed and released while
  ;; E or I is still down.
  ectl (tap-hold-release $tap $hold-mod e lctl)
  ictl (tap-hold-release $tap $hold-mod i rctl)

  ;; From here on, every alias is named after the character it types on an
  ;; ABNT2 layout. The first ones are shifted characters from the digit row.
  ex   S-1       ;; !
  at   S-2       ;; @
  hsh  S-3       ;; hash sign
  dol  S-4       ;; $
  pct  S-5       ;; %
  amp  S-7       ;; &
  ast  S-8       ;; *
  lpar S-9       ;; (
  rpar S-0       ;; )
  und  S--       ;; _
  pls  S-=       ;; +
  col  S-/       ;; colon, since the physical slash key prints a semicolon

  ;; Brackets live on unusual keys on ABNT2: the physical ] key types [ and
  ;; the physical backslash key types ].
  lbkt ]         ;; [
  rbkt bksl      ;; ]
  lbrc S-]       ;; {
  rbrc S-bksl    ;; }

  ;; These four aliases use the custom key names defined in deflocalkeys-linux,
  ;; for the keys that only exist on ABNT2.
  bsl  abntbs            ;; backslash
  pipe S-abntbs          ;; vertical bar
  sl   abntslash         ;; slash
  qst  S-abntslash       ;; question mark

  ;; The key left of 1 types an apostrophe, and a double quote when shifted.
  sq   grv       ;; apostrophe
  dq   S-grv     ;; double quote

  ;; Tilde, circumflex and backtick are dead keys on ABNT2, so they only
  ;; appear after the next key press. Each macro types the dead key followed
  ;; by a space, which produces the plain character on its own.
  til  (macro apos 10 spc)      ;; tilde
  car  (macro S-apos 10 spc)    ;; circumflex
  bt   (macro S-[ 10 spc)       ;; backtick
)

(deflayer base
  q    @wsft @ectl r    t    y    u    @ictl @osft p    [
  @anum s    d    f    g    h    j    k    l    @csym
  z    x    c    v    b    n    m    ,    .    /
)

#|
Hold A to get this layer, which puts a numpad on the right hand and brackets on
the left hand. On the left hand the ring finger types the opening brackets and
the index finger types the closing ones: w, s and x give [, ( and {, while r, f
and v give ], ) and }. On the right hand, u, i and o give 7, 8 and 9, j, k and l
give 4, 5 and 6, and m, comma and period give 1, 2 and 3, with 0 on n and the
decimal point on h. The decimal comma, which is what Brazilian numbers use, is
on g, right next to the decimal point. The remaining right hand keys carry the
operators: y types =, p types *, the key right of p types /, the c-cedilla key
types + and the physical slash key types -. The keys q, e, t, a, c and b keep
typing their letters. The d key does nothing in this layer, so it is mapped to
XX, a no-op.

The z key reloads this configuration file (live reload), so holding A and
pressing z applies any edit without restarting the service. If the new file
has an error, kanata keeps running with the previous configuration and prints
the error in its log.
|#
(deflayer num
  q    @lbkt e   @rbkt t    =    7    8    9    @ast @sl
  a    @lpar XX  @rpar ,    .    4    5    6    @pls
  lrld @lbrc c   @rbrc b    0    1    2    3    -
)

#|
Hold C-cedilla to get this layer, which keeps programming symbols on the left
hand and on the outer keys of the right hand. On the left hand, the top row
types the shifted digits ! @ # $ and %, the home row types { ( ) } followed by
a backtick on g, and the bottom row types a backslash, [, ], * and +. On the
right hand, y, u, o and p type ^, &, a vertical bar and ?, the key right of p
types a slash, the home row keys h, j and l type -, = and _, and n, m, period
and the physical slash key type ~, an apostrophe, a colon and a double quote.

The characters < > and ; are left out of this layer because they are already
easy to reach on the base layer, with Shift and comma, Shift and period, and
the semicolon key itself. The i, k and comma keys and the c-cedilla key do
nothing in this layer, so they are mapped to XX, a no-op.
|#
(deflayer sym
  @ex  @at  @hsh @dol @pct @car @amp XX   @pipe @qst @sl
  @lbrc @lpar @rpar @rbrc @bt -    =    XX   @und XX
  @bsl @lbkt @rbkt @ast @pls @til @sq  XX   @col @dq
)
